.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2317:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1503:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:8:**Consumed by:** [DOS-7](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-7) (`intelligence_claims.actor` column, tombstones), [DOS-241](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-241) (enrichment refactor design)
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2318:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1505:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:15:2. [DOS-7](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-7) elevates `intelligence_claims` to a first-class table with per-claim identity and append-only assertion history.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2319:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1507:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:22:This ADR specifies the actor taxonomy, the propose/commit boundary, the contradiction-handling rules, the agent trust ledger, and the deduplication policy that collectively make humans and agents first-class, equal-structured claim sources. The substrate pieces needed in v1.4.0 are limited: an `actor` column and a `claim_state` column on `intelligence_claims`, plus the propose/commit state machine. The user-facing surfaces (Analysis Inbox, agent trust dashboard, contradiction resolution UI) ship in v1.5.0+ and are out of scope for v1.4.0.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2320:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1508:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:28:Every claim in `intelligence_claims` carries an `actor` string with one of the following canonical shapes. The `actor` column is non-nullable. Provenance's `Actor` type ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §1) uses the same shape.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2321:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1512:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:96:- **Transform abilities** produce `AbilityOutput<ClaimProposal>` — synthesized assertions with full provenance, but no side effect on `intelligence_claims`.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2322:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1517:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:174:  claim_a_id      TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES intelligence_claims(id),
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2323:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1518:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:175:  claim_b_id      TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES intelligence_claims(id),
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2324:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1522:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:209:[ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)'s `DataSource` enum is **not** extended with new variants for actors. `DataSource` describes the *origin of information* (Glean downstream, Clay, Gong transcript, user input). `actor` describes *who asserted the claim*. A single agent claim has `actor = 'agent:prepare_meeting:1.3'` and sources `[Glean { downstream: Salesforce }, Glean { downstream: Gong }]`. The two axes are orthogonal and remain so.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2325:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1523:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:215:- `actor` column on `intelligence_claims` with canonical values from §1.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2326:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1527:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:295:**Consolidation timing — 2026-04-20 update:** Per plan-eng-review AskUserQuestion #1 and the brownfield-as-greenfield founder commitment (D1), consolidation ships in v1.4.0 as a destructive migration alongside the `intelligence_claims` table creation. Original R1.3 deferred consolidation to v1.4.1 out of migration caution; that deferral is retracted. Rationale: at AI velocity + minimal user base, keeping three parallel tombstone mechanisms alive through v1.4.0 creates interop cost that exceeds consolidation cost. The interop is what's expensive; one-table-to-rule-them-all is cleaner.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2327:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1528:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:302:- Reads switch to `intelligence_claims` for all tombstone queries.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2328:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1531:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:317:The original §1 declared actor as a string with canonical shapes. [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §1 defines `Actor` as an enum (`User | Agent | System`). These conflict.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2329:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1532:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:319:**Revised:** `actor` column on `intelligence_claims` stores a **typed representation serialized to string** for schema stability:
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2330:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1535:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:345:  claim_id          TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES intelligence_claims(id),
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2331:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1536:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:353:`corroboration_count` on the claim row becomes a cached denormalization (updated via trigger or on write) for query speed. Provenance of each corroborator is preserved. The trust compiler's corroboration factor reads from this table.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2332:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1537:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:363:- The "three tombstone mechanisms already exist" finding means the migration story is larger than v1.4.0 alone. Flag for [DOS-7](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-7) design review: decide whether v1.4.0 ships the `intelligence_claims` table + schema + service functions but keeps existing tombstone tables authoritative, with full migration to v1.4.1.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2333:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1538:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:364:- `intelligence_claims` does not exist yet — confirmed. [DOS-7](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-7) creates it.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2334:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1540:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:372:- `intelligence_claims` table with schema from [DOS-7](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-7) + `actor`, `claim_state`, `dedup_key`, `corroboration_count` columns.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2335:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1541:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:377:- **Consolidation migrations active** (updated 2026-04-20 per plan-eng-review AskUserQuestion #1): `suppression_tombstones`, `DismissedItem` in-blob entries, and `account_stakeholder_roles.dismissed_at` values all backfilled into `intelligence_claims` as `tombstoned` rows. Reads switch to `intelligence_claims` for tombstone queries. Old tables kept read-only for parity testing.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2336:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:1542:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:394:R1.2 established that supersede vs contradiction is routed by actor identity at read time in `commit_claim`. Persona review flagged that actor-based routing decided at read time + a concurrent second writer produces a race: both writers read "no existing claim at this field_path," both route to supersede, both insert, both hit the unique constraint on `(entity_id, claim_type, field_path, claim_sequence)`, both retry. SQLite's serializable isolation prevents corruption but produces churn and potentially wrong routing decisions.
