.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2415:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-300.log:854:.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:394:R1.2 established that supersede vs contradiction is routed by actor identity at read time in `commit_claim`. Persona review flagged that actor-based routing decided at read time + a concurrent second writer produces a race: both writers read "no existing claim at this field_path," both route to supersede, both insert, both hit the unique constraint on `(entity_id, claim_type, field_path, claim_sequence)`, both retry. SQLite's serializable isolation prevents corruption but produces churn and potentially wrong routing decisions.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2416:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-300.log:857:.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:52:- Any row from any local table: `intelligence_claims`, `signal_events`, `meetings`, `accounts`, `persons`, everything.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2417:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-300.log:858:.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:10:The v1.4.0 claim substrate (DOS-7 `intelligence_claims`, DOS-5 Trust Compiler, DOS-10 freshness decay, DOS-211 Provenance, DOS-212 DataSource taxonomy, DOS-213 prompt fingerprinting, DOS-263 invalidation_jobs, DOS-265 claim_edges, DOS-280 canonicalization) is large, interlocking, and built by multiple contributors over multiple releases. Without a unifying set of invariants, each contributor independently makes small decisions that drift apart over time. The result: two code paths that could in principle do the same thing end up doing subtly different things, ghost-resurrection bugs re-emerge after they were fixed, and every new feature ends up re-solving the "how do I store this?" problem.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2418:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-300.log:859:.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:22:Once a claim is written, its `text`, `claim_type`, `subject_ref`, `source_asof`, and `created_at` are immutable. No UPDATE of those columns from anywhere in the codebase except migration paths.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2419:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-300.log:860:.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:38:`DELETE FROM intelligence_claims` is forbidden outside user-data-deletion flows (GDPR/CCPA). Same for `claim_corroborations` and `claim_contradictions`.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2420:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-300.log:861:.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:42:Enforcement: CI lint grep for `DELETE FROM intelligence_claims|claim_corroborations|claim_contradictions` outside the allowed paths.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-296.log:2421:.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-300.log:864:.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:133:- New features that would be easy to slam into a single column on `intelligence_claims` have to route through the right substrate layer. Some features will take slightly longer to ship as a result; the tradeoff is that the substrate doesn't decay over time.
