     1	# ADR-0110: Evaluation Harness for Abilities
     2	
     3	**Status:** Proposed  
     4	**Date:** 2026-04-18  
     5	**Target:** v1.4.0  
     6	**Extends:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)  
     7	**Depends on:** [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md), [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)  
     8	**Consumed by:** [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §9 (snapshot-diff evaluation), [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §13 (Phase 3 regression gate)
     9	
    10	## Context
    11	
    12	[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §10 Enforcement Phase 3 requires a regression gate before abilities become authoritative; Risk #5 declares the evaluation harness a non-negotiable co-dependency. [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §9 specifies snapshot-diff evaluation for maintenance abilities. [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6 requires replay providers in `Evaluate` mode. [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md) §6 uses fingerprints to classify regression types.
    13	
    14	Without a concrete harness, these requirements do not produce action. This ADR specifies fixture structure, scoring methodology, regression classification, CI integration, and fixture governance (retention, anonymization, source-aware lifecycle).
    15	
    16	## Decision
    17	
    18	### 1. Fixture Shape
    19	
    20	An evaluation fixture captures everything needed to deterministically run an ability against a known input state:
    21	
    22	```
    23	src-tauri/tests/abilities/{ability_name}/
    24	├── fixtures/
    25	│   ├── fixture_001/
    26	│   │   ├── state.sql              # Pre-populated DB state
    27	│   │   ├── inputs.json            # Ability input values
    28	│   │   ├── provider_replay.json   # Recorded provider completions by canonical_prompt_hash
    29	│   │   ├── external_replay.json   # Recorded external API responses (Glean, Slack, etc.)
    30	│   │   ├── clock.txt              # Deterministic clock value
    31	│   │   ├── seed.txt               # RNG seed
    32	│   │   ├── expected_output.json   # Expected ability output
    33	│   │   └── expected_provenance.json # Expected provenance envelope (with field-level attributions)
    34	│   ├── fixture_002/
    35	│   └── manifest.toml              # Fixture metadata, labels, retention
    36	├── evals/
    37	│   ├── quality.toml               # Per-ability quality criteria
    38	│   └── regression_baseline.json   # Baseline scores for regression detection
    39	```
    40	
    41	### 2. Scoring Methodology
    42	
    43	Evaluation produces a score for each fixture run. Scoring differs by ability category:
    44	
    45	**Read abilities** — exact output equality against `expected_output.json`. Field-level diff surfaces any drift. Confidence thresholds: 100% equality required; any diff fails.
    46	
    47	**Transform abilities** — LLM-as-judge scoring via a dedicated judge model. The judge receives the input, the expected output, and the actual output, and produces a rubric score on configurable dimensions (e.g., relevance, faithfulness, attribution completeness). Thresholds are per-ability in `evals/quality.toml`.
    48	
    49	**Maintenance abilities** — snapshot-diff per [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §9. Compare `planned_mutations` against `expected_output.json.planned_mutations`. Diff on operation set, affected entity IDs, and parameter values. Thresholds: exact match or a configured tolerance (e.g., allow timestamp drift of ≤1s).
    50	
    51	**Publish abilities** — the outbox entry is compared, not the external side effect.
    52	
    53	### 3. Regression Classification
    54	
    55	Per [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md) §6, fingerprints classify regressions:
    56	
    57	```rust
    58	pub enum RegressionClass {
    59	    /// Same inputs, same prompt_template_version, different outputs.
    60	    /// Indicates provider drift or nondeterminism in replay.
    61	    ProviderDrift,
    62	    
    63	    /// Same inputs, different prompt_template_version, different outputs.
    64	    /// Expected when a prompt is edited; reviewer approves or rejects.
    65	    PromptChange,
    66	    
    67	    /// Different inputs, same fingerprint otherwise.
    68	    /// Real input-driven behavior change.
    69	    InputChange,
    70	    
    71	    /// canonical_prompt_hash differs but prompt_template_version matches.
    72	    /// Canonicalization bug or unstamped template edit.
    73	    CanonicalizationBug,
    74	    
    75	    /// Output differs and no fingerprint fields explain why.
    76	    /// Indicates a logic change not captured in fingerprint.
    77	    LogicChange,
    78	}
    79	```
    80	
    81	The harness labels each regression with a class. CI treats them differently:
    82	
    83	| Class | CI default action | Reviewer workflow |
    84	|-------|-------------------|-------------------|
    85	| ProviderDrift | Warn | Inspect replay fixture; may refresh or declare as nondeterminism |
    86	| PromptChange | Fail-soft (block merge pending explicit approval) | Reviewer approves by rebaselining; CI re-runs against new baseline |
    87	| InputChange | Fail | Reviewer updates expected output for legitimate input-driven change |
    88	| CanonicalizationBug | Fail (hard) | Must fix canonicalization before merge |
    89	| LogicChange | Fail-soft | Reviewer approves rebaseline or rejects as unintended |
    90	
    91	### 4. CI Integration
    92	
    93	Evaluation runs on every PR that touches `src-tauri/src/abilities/`, prompt template files, or the fixtures themselves. The harness:
    94	
    95	1. Builds the binary with the ability under test.
    96	2. For each fixture in the ability's directory:
    97	   - Constructs `ServiceContext::new_evaluate` with the fixture's clock, seed, replay providers.
    98	   - Loads `state.sql` into an in-memory SQLite.
    99	   - Invokes the ability via the registry with `inputs.json`.
   100	   - Compares actual output, provenance, and planned mutations to expected.
   101	   - Classifies any diffs per §3.
   102	3. Aggregates per-ability scores into a regression report.
   103	4. Compares scores to `regression_baseline.json`; flags any ability whose score drops.
   104	
   105	Runtime budget: evaluation is hermetic (no network), so full ability suite typically runs in 30–60 seconds. Per-ability isolation allows parallel execution.
   106	
   107	### 5. Quality Criteria per Ability
   108	
   109	`evals/quality.toml` defines per-ability thresholds:
   110	
   111	```toml
   112	[prepare_meeting]
   113	judge_model = "claude-sonnet-4-6"
   114	judge_prompt_id = "eval_meeting_brief_judge.v1"
   115	min_relevance = 0.85
   116	min_faithfulness = 0.9
   117	min_attribution_completeness = 0.95
   118	regression_tolerance = 0.02  # 2% score drop is the alert threshold
   119	```
   120	
   121	Judge models invoke a fixture-replayed provider with a dedicated judge prompt that asks for structured rubric scores. Judge outputs are themselves subject to prompt fingerprinting and stored per run for audit.
   122	
   123	### 6. Fixture Authoring and Maintenance
   124	
   125	Fixtures are created two ways:
   126	
   127	- **Hand-authored** for targeted regression cases: author sets up the state, declares the expected output, commits.
   128	- **Captured from Live runs** for coverage: the `--capture-fixture` mode runs an ability in `Live` with a tracing wrapper, records everything the ability saw, and writes a fixture. Captured fixtures must be reviewed before merging.
   129	
   130	Fixture refresh cadence: fixtures capturing live provider output are refreshed when `prompt_template_version` changes, when the replay provider returns outdated completions, or on a quarterly schedule.
   131	
   132	### 7. Fixture Governance: Anonymization, Retention, Purge
   133	
   134	Fixtures can contain PII and source-attributed content. Per [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) and [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md), fixtures must honor source lifecycle:
   135	
   136	**Anonymization rules (applied at capture time):**
   137	
   138	1. Entity names replaced with placeholders (`acct_01`, `person_03`). Mapping preserved in a separate `fixture_identity_map.json` that is NOT committed.
   139	2. Email addresses replaced with `user@example.com`, `person_N@example.com`.
   140	3. Free-text content (email bodies, meeting transcripts, documents) passed through a named-entity redactor; recognizable names, phone numbers, addresses replaced with tokens.
   141	4. Source IDs retained but flagged as "anonymized" so provenance still works; real IDs not present.
   142	
   143	**Retention:**
   144	
   145	- Fixtures with real-anonymized content live in the repo and are retained as long as the ability exists.
   146	- Fixtures are versioned in git; historical fixtures can be reconstructed from git history.
   147	
   148	**Purge on source revocation:**
   149	
   150	- When a user revokes a source, the user's `fixture_identity_map.json` (kept out-of-tree) is consulted; fixtures referencing identities mapped to revoked user data are regenerated or removed.
   151	- Repo-stored anonymized fixtures are not affected because the anonymization is the point of keeping them safely in-tree.
   152	
   153	**Multi-user note for v1.4.0 (six users, local-first):** fixtures are per-developer. Each user's captured fixtures include their own data, anonymized. No shared fixture corpus crosses users without re-anonymization.
   154	
   155	### 8. Regression Gate in Phase 3 Cutover
   156	
   157	[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §13 Phase 3 cutover requires the regression gate to be active before the registry becomes authoritative. The gate's entry requirement is:
   158	
   159	- Every migrated ability has at least one fixture with an expected output.
   160	- `regression_baseline.json` is populated from an accepted baseline run.

