   396	**Revised semantics:** `commit_claim` acquires a **pessimistic row-lock** on the target `(entity_id, claim_type, field_path)` key for the duration of the routing decision + insert. Conceptually:
   397	
   398	```rust
   399	async fn commit_claim(
   400	    ctx: &ServiceContext,
   401	    proposal: ClaimProposal,
   402	) -> Result<CommittedClaim, ClaimError> {
   403	    ctx.check_mutation_allowed()?;
   404	
   405	    let lock_key = (proposal.entity_id, proposal.claim_type.clone(), proposal.field_path.clone());
   406	
   407	    // Acquire exclusive lock on this field_path for the duration of this function.
   408	    // SQLite: either via a dedicated `claim_commit_locks` table with INSERT-RETURNING semantics,
   409	    // or via an in-memory Mutex<HashMap<LockKey, ()>> if we decide app-level locking is enough.
   410	    let _lock = ctx.services.claim_locks.acquire(&lock_key).await?;
   411	
   412	    // Now-uncontested read + routing decision + insert.
   413	    let latest = ctx.services.db.read_latest_claim(&lock_key)?;
   414	    let route = route_for(&proposal, &latest);                // supersede | contradiction | fresh
   415	    apply_route(ctx, route, proposal).await?;
   416	    // _lock drops at function end, releasing the row for other writers.
   417	}
   418	```
   419	
   420	**Lock implementation choices (to decide at DOS-7 design review):**
   421	
   422	- **App-level `Mutex<HashMap<LockKey, ()>>`.** Fast, simple, lives in `services::claims` module. Sufficient for single-process single-user DailyOS. Does not survive a crash — but no lock held across a crash is meaningful anyway. **Recommended default.**
   423	- **Dedicated `claim_commit_locks` table with uniqueness constraint.** DB-backed; survives crashes; higher overhead per commit. Overkill for single-user; potentially useful if multi-process access ever becomes relevant.
   424	- **SQLite `BEGIN EXCLUSIVE` transaction.** Locks the entire DB, not just this field_path. Too coarse.
   425	
   426	The app-level mutex map is the default. If the multi-process scenario emerges (daemon process + Tauri main process both committing claims), revisit.
   427	
   428	**Fairness and starvation:** an active hot field (user rapidly correcting a value) + concurrent agent attempting commits could theoretically queue agent commits indefinitely. Mitigate with a commit-attempt timeout (default 500 ms) returning `Err(ClaimError::LockTimeout)` — caller retries or gives up. User-initiated commits always proceed (locked for short duration); agent commits may time out and be rescheduled.
